out difference; the other is about deficiency. And crucially, even if there were some large-scale trend among lesbians, it still wouldn't justify what you're considering doing to your sister. Because she's not an aggregate statistic. She's a specific person marrying a specific woman. If I told you that men in your age bracket have a certain infidelity rate, would you find it loving if your fiancée's brother pulled you aside before your wedding to warn her about the statistical likelihood of you cheating? Or would you feel pre-judged, reduced to a number, and deeply insulted? That's the position you'd be putting your sister in. Not because the data is necessarily wrong, but because using aggregate patterns to predict and publicly doubt an individual's relationship—especially right before their wedding—is hurtful regardless of the pattern. It says "I see you as a member of a category first, and as a unique human being second." So I'm not saying it's off limits to discuss any pattern. I'm saying the pattern you're claiming isn't supported, and even if it were, it wouldn't justify what you're about to do to your sister.
